@@ -0,0 +1,80 @@
+# Versioned structured memory
+
+`VersionedMemoryRail` is an opt-in openJiuwen `AgentRail` for a fresh
+ReActAgent invocation. Existing Rails and default agent behavior are unchanged.
+The source selector and store use only the Python standard library and can be
+loaded directly by file with `importlib.util`, without initializing JiuwenSwarm.
+
+Records contain `id`, `entity`, `attribute`, `value` (JSON scalar), integer
+`valid_at` and `observed_at`, and `operation` (`set` or `delete`). IDs are unique.
+The store retains source history, including superseded facts and tombstones;
+`records` returns a defensive copy. `append(record)`, `save(path)`, and
+`VersionedMemoryStore.load(path)` support JSONL persistence. Extra annotation
+fields are excluded from source text. This is a structured-input interface;
+it does not extract facts from free text.
+
+A query contains `entity`, `attributes`, `as_of`, and `known_at`. A record is
+visible when both timestamps are within their cutoffs. For each attribute the
+maximal visible `valid_at` determines the applicable evidence. Tied different
+values or set/delete operations are unresolved; deletion, missing evidence,
+and unresolved conflicts should produce null. Selection returns records and
+provenance IDs, never computed final answers.
+
+```python
+from jiuwenswarm.agents.harness.common.versioned_memory import (
+    VersionedMemoryStore, select_records,
+)
+from jiuwenswarm.agents.harness.common.rails import VersionedMemoryRail
+
+selection = select_records(records, query, 'versioned', 512, count_tokens)
+rail = VersionedMemoryRail(
+    records, query, question, 'versioned', 512, count_tokens,
+    selector=select_records, on_response=save_successful_response,
+)
+# Register rail through the agent's normal Rail registration interface.
+```
+
+`select_records(records, query, policy, budget, count_tokens)` returns a mapping
+with `text` (canonical JSONL), `record_ids`, `proxy_tokens`, and `conflicts`.
+The supplied counter measures the entire joined text, including separators.
+The budget covers source context only, not question, metadata, instructions,
+model output, or framework overhead. `full` is intentionally unbounded.
+
+| Policy | Selection |
+| --- | --- |
+| `full` | All supplied source records, in input order; unbounded reference. |
+| `recent` | Contiguous input-order suffix; stops at the first record that cannot fit. |
+| `lexical` | Query-token overlap, including optional query `question`; stable input-order ties. |
+| `bm25` | BM25 with document frequencies, length normalization, k1=1.5 and b=0.75. |
+| `versioned` | Both time cutoffs, entity/attribute filter, maximal valid time per attribute. |
+| `no_time` | Observed cutoff and observed ordering only; ignores valid cutoff/order. |
+| `no_conflict` | Both cutoffs; maximal valid time ties resolved by observed order, then input order. |
+
+Retrieval baselines rank the supplied raw history without temporal filtering;
+all policies receive the same query metadata and temporal interpretation in
+the Rail prompt. Structured policies preserve every tied source record as an
+atomic attribute group. Groups are packed by increasing proxy cost, with query
+attribute order as the tie-break, to favor attribute coverage. A conflict group
+that cannot fit is omitted in full. Packing is greedy and does not promise an
+optimal subset for nonadditive token counters. All choices are deterministic.
+
+The Rail constructor is
+`VersionedMemoryRail(records, query, question, policy, budget, count_tokens, selector=None, on_response=None)`.
+Pass `selector` explicitly when loading the Rail file directly by `importlib`.
+The Rail exposes `selection`, `selected_records` (IDs), `context_text`,
+`responses`, and `model_inputs`. Its optional synchronous `on_response(response,
+messages)` hook runs immediately after each successful model response, before
+later agent postprocessing; exceptions propagate to the caller. It modifies
+only the current invocation query and does not prune persistent history.
+
+Run the offline contract suite from the release repository root:
+
+```console
+python -m unittest discover -s tests -p test_versioned_memory.py
+```
+
+Tests cover historical and knowledge cutoffs, late stale observations,
+conflicts, deletions, partial updates, retrieval and tight budgets, provenance,
+store persistence and Rail prompt/response hooks. They avoid network calls and
+costly package initialization. A real ReActAgent/provider integration check
+belongs to the separately bounded runtime evaluation.
